{"content":{"attachment":{"filename":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/annals/README.md","snippet":"27\t## The envelope\n28\t\n29\t```ts\n30\t{\n31\t  id: \"ev2-<sha256 of the identity subset>\",\n32\t  schema: \"annals/v1\",\n33\t  kind: string,                    // open; owned by (producer.tool, kind)\n34\t  occurred_at, recorded_at: string // ISO 8601; recorded_at not in identity\n35\t  producer: { tool, version? },    // who wrote it; (tool, kind) names a vocabulary\n36\t  meta?: { ... },                  // producer-owned metadata, uninterpreted\n37\t  stream?: { id, seq, parent? },   // grouping + ordering, e.g. a conversation\n38\t  links?: [{ rel, target }],       // typed edges to other records\n39\t  content: unknown,                // never interpreted by annals\n40\t  media_type?, raw?, resolved?, redactions?, context?\n41\t}\n42\t```\n43\t\n44\tThe envelope carries no downstream concepts: no actor, no model, no session.\n45\tA vocabulary that wants them (cledger records who spoke and which model\n46\tserved a turn) puts them in `meta`. Each field answers exactly two\n47\tstore-level questions — is it in the id, and does redaction walk it:\n48\t\n49\t| field | in identity | redaction-walked |\n50\t|---|---|---|\n51\t| kind, occurred_at, stream, media_type, content, links | yes | content yes |\n52\t| meta | **no** | **no** — provenance only, never free text |\n53\t| raw | no | yes (`raw.data`) |\n54\t| producer, context, recorded_at, redactions | no | no |\n55\t| resolved | no | **no** — never put payloads here |\n56\t\n57\t`meta` being identity-excluded is the point: provenance can be added or\n58\tenriched later without the same fact getting a new id. It is not\n59\tredaction-walked — the contract is structured provenance only (ids, names,\n60\tlabels); anything that could carry source text or a secret belongs in\n61\t`content`/`raw`. This matches the pre-extraction behavior (actor/producer\n62\twere never walked) and keeps UUID-heavy metadata out of the entropy scan's\n63\tfalse-positive path. A vocabulary that\n64\tneeds one of its facts *in* identity puts it in `content`. Note the standing\n65\tconsequence: two records differing only by producer or meta dedup to one —\n66\tbyte-identical content is the same fact.\n67\t\n68\t## Namespaces\n69\t\n70\tEvery operation takes a `Ledger` — a repo handle plus a namespace — from\n71\t`openLedger(repo, opts)`. A namespace picks the notes ref\n72\t(`refs/notes/<name>`), the state directory under `.git`, the config file\n73\tname, and the CLI the pre-push hook invokes. Producers that want their own\n74\tindependently-pushable, independently-wipeable ref (a derived layer that must\n75\tbe rebuildable, say) open their own namespace; producers extending an\n76\texisting vocabulary write into that vocabulary's namespace through its\n77\towner's API. cledger uses `{ name: \"conversation-ledger\", configFile: \".cledger.json\" }`.\n78\t\n79\tTwo namespaces in one repo chain two hook blocks in `pre-push`. Git feeds\n80\tthat script its stdin once, so only the first block sees the pushed-ref\n81\tlist; later blocks degrade to `HEAD` scope — records still push, scoped to\n82\tthe checked-out branch. And a custom namespace's hook only fires if its\n83\t`cliName` is on PATH or `hookInvocation` was given; otherwise its owner\n84\tsyncs through the library.\n85\t\n86\t## Size policy\n87\t\n88\tRecords carry pointers, digests, and spans — never large artifacts. Git notes\n89\treplicate to every clone that fetches the ref; a checkpoint, a media file, or\n90\ta whole database belongs elsewhere, referenced by path + hash (see\n91\t`resolved`). The redaction stack walks `content` and `raw.data` only, which\n92\tis the second reason not to smuggle payloads into other fields. Enforced\n93\tsoftly: an event serializing over `limits.warnEventBytes` (default 5MB)\n94\twarns on stderr but still appends — capture must never drop records on size\n95\talone; set `limits.maxEventBytes` to opt into hard refusal for a producer\n96\tthat would rather fail loudly, such as a derived layer.\n97\t\n98\t## Identity records (optional pattern)\n99\t\n100\tThe envelope has no actor field, and attribution does not need one: an\n101\tidentity can be a record like any other — its own kind, its own producer —\n102\tand a record claims authorship with a link (`{ rel: \"authored_by\", target:\n103\t<identity record id> }`). That is how git-bug stores identities, versioned\n104\tand signable, in the same store as its bugs. Nothing in annals mandates\n105\tthis; a vocabulary can just as well denormalize `meta.actor` onto every\n106\trecord (cledger does today) and adopt identity records later without a\n107\tschema change — both are producer-space conventions.\n108\t\n109\t## Prior art\n110\t\n111\t[git-bug's `entity/dag`](https://github.com/git-bug/git-bug) (Go) is the\n112\tclosest relative — replicated append-only operations in git refs, id = hash\n113\tof the serialized op, per-type `{namespace, typename, formatVersion}`; its\n114\tidentification triple matches annals' `(producer.tool, kind,\n115\tproducer.version)`. Dolt's git-remote support solves an overlapping problem\n116\twith a versioned SQL database; it lacks commit anchoring, which is the","type":"edited_text_file"},"injection_type":"attachment/edited_text_file","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/annals/README.md changed on disk since you last read it. That's usually deliberate, so take it as the current state rather than reverting it; if the change looks wrong, say so rather than undoing it yourself — otherwise no need to call it out. Here are the relevant changes (shown with line numbers):\n27\t## The envelope\n28\t\n29\t```ts\n30\t{\n31\t  id: \"ev2-<sha256 of the identity subset>\",\n32\t  schema: \"annals/v1\",\n33\t  kind: string,                    // open; owned by (producer.tool, kind)\n34\t  occurred_at, recorded_at: string // ISO 8601; recorded_at not