{"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Fixed, verified, and pushed to both repos. Since your `cledger` is npm-linked to this checkout, the fix is already live on your machine — nothing to install.\n\n## What happened in `resume`, and what to do\n\nThe pre-push hooks installed in your repos before the extraction check `$CLEDGER_INTERNAL` to know \"this push is cledger's own notes push, don't re-enter.\" The extracted code derived that guard name from the namespace (`CONVERSATION_LEDGER_INTERNAL`), so old hooks never saw it: push → hook → transport-push → internal notes push → hook again → … with a full secret scan at every level (your 2h05m), levels racing each other's pushes (the `cannot lock ref … expected …` error), error messages nesting inside each other (the `)))))` pile), and GitHub dropping connections under the hammering. Hooks only upgrade themselves when a repo *captures* something — never on push — so any repo that pushed before its next capture was exposed. `resume` was the first one to try.\n\nThe fix pins the guard name as a namespace field (`CLEDGER_INTERNAL`, exactly like the earlier `cledger-incoming` fix), and `transport-push` now also exits immediately if it sees its own guard — so any future naming drift costs one wasted call, not a storm. I reproduced your exact scenario in a smoke test (old hook text, existing notes, push with no prior capture): 1-second push, hook fired twice (your push + the internal one, which no-ops), notes delivered, zero recursion.\n\nFor `resume` itself: just run `git push origin main` again — it should complete in seconds and carry the notes along. This was the second regression of the same family, so I did the full inventory of every name the old installs baked into durable state — notes ref, fetch refspec, hook marker text, guard env var, state dir, config file paths, and the notes merge-strategy config key — all seven are now explicitly pinned in cledger's namespace; there's nothing else of that shape left to bite.\n\n## Ordering: send the turnbridge prompt now\n\nNo need to wait for the cleanup. The cleanup PR only touches cledger internals (pipe-through files, the wipe script, ev1 tolerance) — none of it changes the surface turnbridge codes against. The only coupling is the `ConversationRef` alias, which the cleanup will simply keep until turnbridge's branch lands. The one thing I *would* do first is merge PR #11 (it now also carries this recursion fix, and the turnbridge agent's step 0 has it rebasing/pushing against a settled main) — merge commit, not squash, for the anchor-preservation reason from last time. So: approve/merge PR #11 → hand the agent its prompt → we do the cleanup PR in parallel whenever, with the wipe before the ev1-tolerance half.\n\nresult: The resume push storm was a real extraction bug — old installed hooks check CLEDGER_INTERNAL but the new code set a namespace-derived guard name, so notes pushes re-entered the hook recursively; fixed by pinning the guard name per namespace plus an in-process breaker, smoke-verified against the exact scenario, pushed to annals and PR #11, live install already patched (re-push resume normally). Turnbridge prompt can go out now — just merge PR #11 first; cleanup proceeds independently.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"annals-extraction","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"f4054182c790a44d2156d92f439ed2e9e5e2496f","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"id":"ev2-57707147917ed9df520fdb0135675c6566c5a294e099a9c0c478a2767e2fc643","kind":"conversation_turn","meta":{"actor":{"id":"claude-fable-5","type":"agent"},"model":"claude-fable-5","session_id":"a044dbb5-5e38-4400-bb2a-97061ee33f87","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.277"},"occurred_at":"2026-09-18T21:13:41.926Z","producer":{"tool":"cledger","version":"0.26.0"},"raw":{"data":{"advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","apiBlockIndex":1,"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-led